## What's litefunctions

Kubernetes-native platform for sync + async workloads. No vendor lock-in, no language lock-in.

## Let's see it in action

Live demo of the runtime console and operational flow.

## How does it work

Control plane in your cluster. Source-of-truth in your repo. Runtime choices open.

## Lambda Architecture

Traditional serverless: client → gateway → functions. One size fits all. Restricted by platform.

## LiteFunctions Architecture

Client → Ingestor → Operator → Runtimes. Control plane in-cluster. Git-driven updates.

## VCS Integration

Push to repo triggers webhook. Ingestor notifies Operator. Runtime refreshes from VCS. No rebuilds.

## Scenarios - Static vs Dynamic

Static: Go/Rust. Compiled, containerized, rebuild required. Dynamic: Python/TS/Lua. Git pull, no rebuild.

## Scenarios - Sync vs Async

Sync: HTTP. Immediate response. Request/response pattern. Async: NATS pub/sub. Event-driven. Background processing.

## Where does go come in

Go powers the operator, ingestor, and runtime. The engine room of the platform.

## The Goth Stack

Go + Templ + HTMX. Minimal dependencies. Fast iteration. Production ready.

## Kubernetes Operator

Manages Function CRDs. Reconciles deployments. Keeps runtimes alive. Kubebuilder + Cobra.

## Ingestor

Routes HTTP requests. Publishes to NATS. Handles sync + async. Entry point for all traffic.

## Runtimes

Go, Rust, Python, TS, Lua. Multi-language execution. Dynamic refresh for non-static langs.

## SQLc

Type-safe SQL queries in Go. Generated code, no runtime overhead.

## WebAuthn

Passwordless authentication. Hardware security keys. FIDO2/WebAuthn standard.

## What needs to change

Traditional FaaS: vendor lock-in, language lock-in, rebuild cycles. LiteFunctions: own your control plane.

## Summary

Full control. Multi-language. Git-driven. Kubernetes-native. No vendor lock-in.

